== Ten Rules for the Rookie

These ten habits carry you further than any build. Read them before your first big hunt; re-read them after your first wall.

1. *Eat dango before every quest.* It is free stats and a free food skill. Forgetting it is the most common avoidable death.
2. *Master one weapon before trying another.* Every weapon is a full game. Pick one, learn it in the training area, and only branch out when hunts feel easy.
3. *Keep your armor upgraded.* Armor spheres are cheap and defense is the difference between carting and surviving. Upgrade before every urgent.
4. *Collect tracks to track.* The monster's icon on the map is a tool, not a guess. If you lose the monster, gather tracks instead of wandering.
5. *Learn to wirefall.* Knocked down? Recover instantly with the wirebug action. It is the single most life-saving input in the game.
6. *Break parts, don't just DPS.* Breaking parts staggers the monster and grants extra rewards — aim for the head, tail, and claws on purpose.
7. *Capture when you can.* Trap + two tranq bombs ends the hunt early with better rewards. It is the efficient, kind finish.
8. *Carry a full item loadout.* Potions, mega potions, whetstone, antidotes — save it as a loadout so it is one button before every quest.
9. *Gather as you run.* Honey, herbs, ores, and spiribirds make every hunt self-funding. Stop treating gathering as a side quest.
10. *Failure is data.* Every cart teaches a tell. The hunters who clear the endgame are not the ones who never fainted — they are the ones who learned from it.
